# CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION {#sec-ch1-introduction}

## Background and Business Problem(s)

The Collins College of Hospitality Management (CCHM) at Cal Poly Pomona offers a hands-on hospitality education with several resources and experiences that help set it apart from other programs. These include a teaching restaurant that is incorporated into the curriculum through the PolyX experience, specialty courses focused on wine, spirits, and beer culture, a wine auditorium and library, off-campus industry visits, and a large alumni network throughout Southern California’s hospitality industry. The college also receives direct support from its Dean’s Board of Advisors, which includes hospitality industry executives. In addition, the annual Hospitality Uncorked gala raises more than one million dollars in a single evening, with much of the funding going toward merit-based student scholarships.

Despite these strengths, CCHM enrollment has declined from approximately 1,204 students in 2015–16 to about 434 students in 2025–26, while overall enrollment at Cal Poly Pomona has remained relatively steady at around 28,000 students. Many current students also have limited involvement in college events and activities outside the classroom, which may create challenges for retention. At the same time, the college’s communication channels do not consistently highlight many of the experiences and resources that make the program unique. This creates two connected business problems: CCHM needs to attract more new students while also doing a better job of engaging and retaining the students who are already enrolled.

## Project Objectives (POs)

- **PO1.** Increase CCHM enrollment toward the Dean’s goal of approximately 600 students.
- **PO2.** Improve student retention by increasing engagement and sense of belonging among current students, especially first-time freshmen.
- **PO3.** Increase prospective-student awareness of CCHM’s unique programs, resources, and experiences through a content strategy connected to campus and industry events.

## Analytics Objectives

### AO1. Measure engagement (views, saves, shares, follower growth) on Collins College Instagram Reels and Stories, and compare it against student engagement, to determine whether social media community-building content correlates with stronger student retention among hospitality students.(Ruben Garcia) {#sec-ao1-title-ao1}

AO1 will examine the positive psychology of posts in Instagram Stories and Reels, specifically how highlighting the purpose and community the Collins School of Hospitality offers, through ad space, clubs promotion through stories, and the student experience in reels, contributes to student retention.

As the marketing researcher, tools utilized will include: IRB form for students interviews including: focus groups with hospitality undergraduates and one-on-one interviews with leaders of CMAA as well as alumni.

The purpose of these interviews is to identify the factors that foster hospitality students' sense of belonging and examine their experiences holistically, illuminating aspects of the program that are underrepresented in current social media outreach. This approach also seeks to surface challenges within the program so that current students' concerns are better understood and addressed (Anthony et al., 2023). Findings from these interviews will inform Reels and Stories content that highlights the experiences students most value, while presenting challenging aspects of the program in a more approachable way.

Quantitative data will be drawn from Google Analytics 4 and analytics provided by Professor Kwok and the Collins College Instagram account, supplemented by qualitative insights from Instagram engagement and student attendance at campus events (Rodriguez, Gil, and Maupomé, 2025). Together, these sources will help determine whether social media community-building through Reels and Stories corresponds with stronger engagement and retention among hospitality students.

This objective will help us understand the psychological drivers behind targeted content, such as belonging, a secure career path, and being part of something larger than a degree, and how emphasizing these themes in ongoing social media engagement (Reels and Stories) strengthens hospitality students' sense of community and likelihood of persisting in the program (Bae, Park, and Kim, 2023).

### AO2. Identify factors that may explain differences in retention between first-time freshman and transfer students at the Collins College of Hospitality Management. (Nik Sahami)

AO2 addresses the gap in retention rates between first-time freshmen and transfer students as observed by the faculty in the Collins College of Hospitality Management, with transfer students apparently having a much higher rate. This creates an opportunity to investigate what factors may contribute to the difference and whether those factors can help develop retention strategies moving forward. Previous research specifically examining hospitality and tourism students found that curriculum, commitment to the hospitality and tourism degree, student life, and financial support were associated with students' intention to stay in their program (Bae, Park, and Kim, 2023). This suggests that retention within hospitality programs may be influenced by factors beyond student type alone.

The dependent variable for this objective would be the student retention rates, and the main independent variable would be the student type, either transfer or first-time freshman. To investigate this relationship, I will be evaluating this relationship using a number of other independent variables including involvement in extracurricular activities, participation in college events, engagement, financial factors, and student demographics, in order to understand the associations behind the difference in retention rates. These variables are consistent with previous hospitality education research identifying student life and financial support as factors associated with students' intention to remain in their programs (Bae, Park, and Kim, 2023). Also, other research has approached student retention through a relationship marketing perspective, suggesting that strengthening the relationship between students and their school can be another way of understanding and addressing retention (Ackerman and Schibrowsky, 2007).

First, I will measure the actual gap in retention rates using the data provided by CCHM. I will then conduct surveys and interviews to measure type of student and their position in the previously mentioned metrics. Hospitality studies have also examined whether factors affecting satisfaction and intention to stay are different between students at different stages of their education, highlighting the importance of measuring differences in transfer students and first-time freshmen (Bae, Park, and Kim, 2023). By collecting this data, I aim to determine how much retention differs by entry pathway and identify which measurable factors are associated with that difference.

### AO3. Identify where CCHM loses prospective students between application and enrollment, and which factors most influence the decision to enroll, using admissions data and a survey of current students. (Aldo Naranjo)

AO3 examines the final stage of the enrollment funnel, from application to enrollment. This places AO3 in the choice phase of college choice, when an admitted student decides which institution to attend, and picks up where AO4's focus on the predisposition and search phases ends (Hossler & Gallagher, 1987). This objective supports PO1 by showing where in the admission process potential enrollment is being lost and what persuades the students who do enroll.

The analysis will use CCHM's counts of applications received, students admitted, and students who accepted and enrolled, by term and by applicant profile, to calculate conversion rates at each stage and identify where the largest drops occur. A survey of current CCHM students will then ask what drew them to apply, what nearly led them elsewhere, and which other programs they considered. Prior research on hospitality students found that their choice rested more on the reputation and offerings of the specific hospitality program than on the university as a whole, and that parents, counselors, and peers also shaped the decision (O'Mahony et al., 2001), so the survey will measure both program level and personal influences.

Expected insights include the stage where CCHM loses the most admitted students and a ranked list of the factors that most shaped enrolled students' decisions. These findings will help CCHM decide which messages, contact points, and program assets to emphasize with admitted students, and will inform the content strategy in PO3.

### AO4.Determining which digital channels and content themes most effectively generate prospective-student awareness and inquiry for the Collins College of Hospitality Management. \[work in progress\] (Jake Evans)

AO4 focuses on identifying which digital channels and content themes are most effective at generating awareness and inquiry among prospective students for the Collins College. This places AO4 in the predisposition and search phases of college choice, before a prospective student applies (Hossler & Gallagher, 1987). The dependent variable is inquiry, which is measured through actions that show interest in learning more about the college, including intake form submissions, referred sessions to the Bronco Applicant Portal, engaged sessions on program information pages, event registrations, and email list subscriptions. The independent variables include channel and medium, content theme, creative format, and publication timing. These variables follow prior research showing that content category, creative format, and posting timing affect engagement with university social media (Peruta & Shields, 2018).

Measurement will use GA4 with consistent UTM tracking, native analytics from the college's social platforms, paid advertising data, email performance metrics, and an IRB approved survey of prospective students. Each campaign post will be tagged so we can connect inquiries back to the post that generated them.

Inquiry will remain the main dependent variable and primary data for AO4. As secondary data, the Collins College will also provide aggregate application, admission, and enrollment counts from before and after the campaign. This will let us compare the full funnel from inquiry to application, admission, and enrollment across both periods while still keeping inquiry as the main focus of the analysis.

The final deliverable will show which channels and which of the college's unique assets generate the strongest response from prospective students. This should help the Collins College decide where to focus its communication efforts and how to better use its paid media budget.

## Significance of the Topic

Enrollment plays an important role in CCHM’s financial health, faculty resources, and ability to prepare graduates for careers in the hospitality industry. If enrollment continues to decline, it could create long-term challenges for the college, even though Southern California continues to offer strong career opportunities in hospitality.

This project is important because it approaches enrollment and retention as connected issues rather than separate problems. Many of the experiences that help current students feel engaged and connected to the college are also the experiences that can be promoted to prospective students. By combining student research, retention data, academic performance analysis, and digital channel measurement, the project can provide CCHM with evidence-based recommendations about what information to communicate, which audiences to focus on, and which channels are most effective. The goal is to help the college increase enrollment while also improving the experience of the students it already serves.

